  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Requirements**\n\nI\'m making good progress! I\'ve started by meticulously dissecting the user\'s requirements, focusing on the first element: the \"\345\244\232\346\242\235\344\273\266\350\244\207\345\220\210\347\257\251\351\201\270\351\235\242\346\235\277.\" I\'ve confirmed that the critical feature of instant keyword search is supported, which is a key part of the design. My current focus is to ensure all elements are clearly defined.\n\n\n**Defining Filter Parameters**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the numerical filter boxes, mapping each to its corresponding parameter \342\200\223 `minCashYield`, `minTotalYield`, and so on. I\'m verifying the \"Yes\" mapping for each, and the logic is sound. I\'ve also confirmed the presence of \"Apply Filters,\" \"Clear Conditions,\" and CSV export buttons. I\'m moving onto the 23-column table specifications, focusing on sticky headers and columns.\n\n\n**Specifying Column Functionality**\n\nI\'ve determined each column header needs to be clickable to enable sorting in ascending or descending order. This feature will use \342\226\262\342\226\274 arrows to indicate the sorting direction. Furthermore, I\'ve outlined the technical architecture, including an `stocksData` array and a `/public/stocks.json` file. My plan is for a Python script to update the JSON file automatically. I also have a preliminary list of 12 stocks and ETFs to use.\n\n\n"
